blueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:525:	Since an $\ell$-level CL function is also $k$-level conditionally linear for all $k \ge \ell$, it suffices to prove the claim when $\ell_j = \ell$ for all $j$. Proceed by induction on $\ell$. For $\ell = 0$ every $L^{(j)}$ is the zero function on $V^{(j)}$, so $L$ is the zero function on $V$, which is the unique $0$-level CL function on $V$ (Definition~\ref{def:cl-func}). For the inductive step with $\ell \ge 1$, decompose each $L^{(j)}$ into its first-level linear part $L^{(j)}_1$ on $V^{(j)}_1$ and its family of $(\ell-1)$-level remainders $L^{(j)}_{>1,\,v_j}$ on $V^{(j)}_{>1}$. The direct sums of the first-level parts define a linear map on $V_1 = \bigoplus_j V^{(j)}_1$, and, for each prefix value $v = \sum_j v_j$, the direct sums of the remainders are $(\ell-1)$-level conditionally linear on $V_{>1} = \bigoplus_j V^{(j)}_{>1}$ by the induction hypothesis. This exhibits $L$ in the form required by Definition~\ref{def:cl-func}.
blueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:530:	For each $i \in \{1, \dots, m\}$ let $L^{(i)}, R^{(i)} \colon V^{(i)} \to V^{(i)}$ be $\ell_i$-level conditionally linear functions, and let $L, R \colon V \to V$ be the direct sums $L = \bigoplus_i L^{(i)}$ and $R = \bigoplus_i R^{(i)}$, as defined in Lemma~\ref{lem:cl-func-prod}. Then the conditionally linear distribution $\mu_{L,R}$ is the product distribution $\prod_{i=1}^m \mu_{L^{(i)},\,R^{(i)}}$ over $V \times V$.
